>I don't know what Martin meant by an anticlock, but I notice that this
>also makes it very hard to overcome a very low attribute score.

A clock encourages (or forces, depending on its character) the player to
make haste. By "anticlock", I mean a mechanic with the opposite effect:
It encourages the player to procrastinate.

Exercise permits the improving of stats over time; making it require
multiple points of stat gain to actually gain a stat point is hence an
anticlock.

>I think the way to handle ability scores is basically to do what D&D
>3rd edition did, remove (or at least raise) the cap and reduce bonuses
>a bit.

3rd edition made it much easier to get *a* bonus (since you only need a
12 in a stat to get a bonus from it, where most stats in 1st and 2nd
edition gave little direct benefit until about 15-16), and had
relatively little effect on the benefits of a score of 18.
